# Geoscape markers.
#
# Eight Cobra site types used to share markerIcon 7 — a front company, a
# factory, a research lab and a Terror Drome were the same dot. These are the
# replacements, plus the Joe side and the player's own annotation layer.
#
# WHY THESE ARE 72px IN A 3x3 SET
#
# Mod.cpp creates GlobeMarkers as `new SurfaceSet(3, 3)`, but a frame does not
# have to be the set's size: Surface::loadImage nulls the surface and rebuilds
# it at the PNG's own dimensions, and Globe::drawTarget centres on the marker's
# own width and height:
#
#     ShaderMove<const Uint8> surf{ marker, x - marker->getWidth() / 2,
#                                           y - marker->getHeight() / 2 };
#
# So an oversized frame works and lands centred. The 3x3 is the vanilla art,
# not a ceiling.
#
# WHY THE INDICES START AT 9
#
# GlobeMarkers has maxSharedFrames = 9, so vanilla owns 0-8. Both sides of the
# lookup shift anything above that by the mod's own offset, and they agree:
#
#   ExtraSprites::getFrame   index >= maxSharedFrames (9)  ->  index + offset
#   AlienDeployment::load    mod->getOffset(markerIcon, 8) ->  index + offset
#
# which is why `markerIcon: 9` below resolves to the frame written as 9 here.
# Do not pre-add an offset by hand in either place.
#
# WHY ONLY FOUR COLOURS
#
# The globe draws in the 8-bit geoscape palette, so these are indexed PNGs on
# PALETTES.DAT palette 0 and every pixel is one of four entries that exist in
# it exactly — nothing is quantised at load:
#
#   13  (252,0,0)      Cobra
#  144  (176,148,68)   gold accent; the only true warm gold in this palette
#  245  (128,188,228)  G.I. Joe
#    5  (252,252,252)  white
#
# Each one is also chosen for its NEIGHBOUR, because drawTarget blinks a marker
# by drawing `srcStuff + 1`. Index 14 was the obvious red and index 15 is pure
# black, so a marker on 14 half-vanishes on every blink. 13 blinks to 14, a
# darker red. 144->145 and 245->246 barely move; 5->6 is the grey vanilla
# already blinks to.
#
# 161 (144,168,168) WAS a fifth, meant to carry "the player has not confirmed
# this". It is gone, and nothing should put it back at this size. A pale
# grey-cyan blended against transparent and reduced 15.4:1 lands nearer WHITE
# or nearer JOE blue than it lands on itself, so a tier drawn in it was
# decided by rounding: pin_rumour came out of the generator as six white
# pixels, and pin_unknown's grey ring arrived with five blue ones in it.
# Confidence is carried by the SHAPE instead — solid head, hollow head, broken
# head — which survives the reduce and the blink both.
#
# Antialiasing is snapped, not quantised, and each icon may only snap to the
# colours it actually drew with (`uses=` in the generator). Searching all of
# them let a gold line's reduce overshoot onto WHITE, which is how an all-gold
# pin picked up twelve white specks.
#
# Regenerate with build/build_globe_markers.py. It prints a warning for any
# icon that comes out with a colour in single figures or under 24 pixels of
# ink, because both mean the art did not survive the reduce.

extraSprites:
  - type: GlobeMarkers
    width: 28
    height: 28
    files:
      # --- Cobra, standing sites. These are the eight that shared icon 7.
      9: Resources/Globe/marker_09_cobra_front_company.png
      10: Resources/Globe/marker_10_cobra_factory.png
      11: Resources/Globe/marker_11_cobra_camp.png
      12: Resources/Globe/marker_12_cobra_depot.png
      13: Resources/Globe/marker_13_cobra_lab.png
      14: Resources/Globe/marker_14_cobra_drome.png
      15: Resources/Globe/marker_15_cobra_broadcast.png
      16: Resources/Globe/marker_16_cobra_hq.png
      # --- Cobra, things that move or happen
      17: Resources/Globe/marker_17_cobra_flight.png
      18: Resources/Globe/marker_18_cobra_craft.png
      19: Resources/Globe/marker_19_cobra_crash.png
      20: Resources/Globe/marker_20_cobra_landed.png
      21: Resources/Globe/marker_21_cobra_terror.png
      22: Resources/Globe/marker_22_cobra_convoy.png
      # --- G.I. Joe. 25 is a fallback only; craft art replaces it.
      23: Resources/Globe/marker_23_joe_pit.png
      24: Resources/Globe/marker_24_joe_base.png
      25: Resources/Globe/marker_25_joe_craft.png
      26: Resources/Globe/marker_26_joe_lz.png
      27: Resources/Globe/marker_27_joe_extract.png
      # --- The player's own board, drawn by Globe::drawMarks and placed with
      #     CTRL + right-click. The three pins are ONE OBJECT three ways —
      #     solid head, hollow head, broken head — so they read as the same
      #     pin at three levels of certainty. gijoe_globe_marks.rul lists
      #     these frames in that order under `globe: markIcons:`, and
      #     Globe::MARK_ICONS indexes it, so the ORDER HERE IS LOAD-BEARING:
      #     confirmed, likely, rumour, note, unknown, route start, waypoint,
      #     area. A lead the game gave the player that has gone stale is drawn
      #     one tier down this list without the stored mark being rewritten.
      28: Resources/Globe/marker_28_pin_confirmed.png
      29: Resources/Globe/marker_29_pin_likely.png
      30: Resources/Globe/marker_30_pin_rumour.png
      31: Resources/Globe/marker_31_pin_note.png
      32: Resources/Globe/marker_32_pin_unknown.png
      33: Resources/Globe/marker_33_route_start.png
      34: Resources/Globe/marker_34_route_waypoint.png
      35: Resources/Globe/marker_35_area_interest.png
